          "text": "<task-notification>\n<task-id>a6f6d76</task-id>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Research Nix Rust project setup\" completed</summary>\n<result>I now have comprehensive information across all five topics. Let me compile the final answer.\n\n---\n\n# Rust + Nix Best Practices (2025-2026)\n\n## 1. Crane vs Naersk: Use Crane\n\n**Crane** (by ipetkov) is the clear recommendation for 2025-2026 Rust builds in Nix. Naersk is older, less actively maintained, and lacks crane's key architectural advantage: **splitting dependency builds from source builds** via `buildDepsOnly` / `cargoArtifacts`. This means changing your source code only rebuilds your code, not all dependencies.\n\nCrane also provides first-class support for Clippy checks (`cargoClippy`), format checks (`cargoFmt`), and a built-in `devShell` helper.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rust Toolchain: oxalica/rust-overlay (Recommended over Fenix)\n\nThe **devenv** project explicitly replaced fenix with **oxalica/rust-overlay** (PR #1500) because rust-overlay is better maintained. Both work, but rust-overlay is the community-preferred choice as of 2025.\n\nKey advantage of rust-overlay: you get `rust-bin.stable.latest.default.override { extensions = [...]; }` which gives you a single derivation containing rustc, cargo, clippy, rustfmt, rust-src, and rust-analyzer. No need for separate packages.\n\nIf you need nightly or a `rust-toolchain.toml` file, fenix has slightly better ergonomics for `fromToolchainFile`. But for stable toolchains, rust-overlay is simpler and more reliable.\n\n---\n\n## 3. flake-utils vs flake-parts vs DIY\n\nThere is a growing recommendation to **avoid both helper libraries** and define your own `forAllSystems` function. Determinate Systems endorses this approach. However, `flake-utils` remains pragmatic for smaller projects, and `flake-parts` is better for large, modular configurations. The examples below use `flake-utils` for brevity, with a DIY alternative shown.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Complete Example Configurations\n\n### Example A: Single Crate with Crane + rust-overlay (Recommended Baseline)\n\nThis is the most common pattern for a single Rust project:\n\n```nix\n{\n  description = \"Rust project built with crane and rust-overlay\";\n\n  inputs = {\n    nixpkgs.url = \"github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable\";\n    crane.url = \"github:ipetkov/crane\";\n    rust-overlay = {\n      url = \"github:oxalica/rust-overlay\";\n      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\";\n    };\n    flake-utils.url = \"github:numtide/flake-utils\";\n  };\n\n  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, crane, rust-overlay, flake-utils, ... }:\n    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:\n      let\n        pkgs = import nixpkgs {\n          inherit system;\n          overlays = [ rust-overlay.overlays.default ];\n        };\n\n        # Stable Rust toolchain with all dev extensions\n        rustToolchain = pkgs.rust-bin.stable.latest.default.override {\n          extensions = [\n            \"rust-src\"       # Required for rust-analyzer \"go to definition\" on std\n            \"rust-analyzer\"\n            \"clippy\"\n            \"rustfmt\"\n          ];\n        };\n\n        # Wire crane to use our specific toolchain\n        craneLib = (crane.mkLib pkgs).overrideToolchain rustToolchain;\n\n        # Filter source to only Rust-relevant files (improves caching)\n        src = craneLib.cleanCargoSource ./.;\n\n        # Common arguments shared between dep-build and final build\n        commonArgs = {\n          inherit src;\n          strictDeps = true;\n\n          # Add any native build inputs your project needs\n          nativeBuildInputs = with pkgs; [\n            pkg-config\n          ];\n          buildInputs = with pkgs; [\n            openssl\n          ] ++ pkgs.lib.optionals pkgs.stdenv.isDarwin [\n            pkgs.darwin.apple_sdk.frameworks.Security\n            pkgs.darwin.apple_sdk.frameworks.SystemConfiguration\n          ];\n        };\n\n        # Build *only* dependencies (cached independently of your source)\n        cargoArtifacts = craneLib.buildDepsOnly commonArgs;\n\n        # Build the final binary\n        my-crate = craneLib.buildPackage (commonArgs // {\n          inherit cargoArtifacts;\n        });\n      in\n      {\n        # `nix flake check` runs all of these\n        checks = {\n          inherit my-crate;\n\n          clippy = craneLib.cargoClippy (commonArgs // {\n            inherit cargoArtifacts;\n            cargoClippyExtraArgs = \"--all-targets -- -D warnings\";\n          });\n\n          fmt = craneLib.cargoFmt {\n            inherit src;\n          };\n\n          # Optional: run tests as a separate check\n          tests = craneLib.cargoNextest (commonArgs // {\n            inherit cargoArtifacts;\n          });\n        };\n\n        packages = {\n          default = my-crate;\n        };\n\n        apps.default = flake-utils.lib.mkApp {\n          drv = my-crate;\n        };\n\n        # Development shell: `nix develop`\n        devShells.default = craneLib.devShell {\n          # Checks to make available in the shell\n          checks = self.checks.${system};\n\n          # Additional dev tools\n          packages = with pkgs; [\n            cargo-watch\n            cargo-edit\n            cargo-expand\n          ];\n        };\n      }\n    );\n}\n```\n\n**Key patterns to note:**\n\n- `craneLib.cleanCargoSource` filters out non-Rust files so that changing README.md does not trigger a rebuild.\n- `buildDepsOnly` compiles all dependencies into a cached artifact. Subsequent `buildPackage` calls reuse it, so only your source code is recompiled.\n- `craneLib.devShell` automatically makes the toolchain (rustc, cargo, rust-analyzer, clippy, rustfmt) available because it inherits the toolchain from `craneLib`.\n- The `checks` attribute means `nix flake check` runs clippy, fmt, and tests in CI.\n\n---\n\n### Example B: Workspace with Multiple Binaries\n\nFor a Cargo workspace with structure:\n\n```\nCargo.toml          (workspace root)\ncrates/\n  server/Cargo.toml\n  cli/Cargo.toml\n  shared/Cargo.toml (library)\n```\n\n```nix\n{\n  description = \"Rust workspace with multiple binaries\";\n\n  inputs = {\n    nixpkgs.url = \"github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable\";\n    crane.url = \"github:ipetkov/crane\";\n    rust-overlay = {\n      url = \"github:oxalica/rust-overlay\";\n      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\";\n    };\n    flake-utils.url = \"github:numtide/flake-utils\";\n  };\n\n  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, crane, rust-overlay, flake-utils, ... }:\n    flake-utils.lib.eachDefaultSystem (system:\n      let\n        pkgs = import nixpkgs {\n          inherit system;\n          overlays = [ rust-overlay.overlays.default ];\n        };\n\n        rustToolchain = pkgs.rust-bin.stable.latest.default.override {\n          extensions = [ \"rust-src\" \"rust-analyzer\" \"clippy\" \"rustfmt\" ];\n        };\n\n        craneLib = (crane.mkLib pkgs).overrideToolchain rustToolchain;\n\n        src = craneLib.cleanCargoSource ./.;\n\n        commonArgs = {\n          inherit src;\n          strictDeps = true;\n          nativeBuildInputs = with pkgs; [ pkg-config ];\n          buildInputs = with pkgs; [ openssl ]\n            ++ pkgs.lib.optionals pkgs.stdenv.isDarwin [\n              pkgs.darwin.apple_sdk.frameworks.Security\n              pkgs.darwin.apple_sdk.frameworks.SystemConfiguration\n            ];\n        };\n\n        # Dependencies are built ONCE for the entire workspace\n        cargoArtifacts = craneLib.buildDepsOnly (commonArgs // {\n          pname = \"my-workspace-deps\";\n        });\n\n        # Helper to build individual workspace binaries\n        buildWorkspaceBin = pname: craneLib.buildPackage (commonArgs // {\n          inherit cargoArtifacts pname;\n          cargoExtraArgs = \"--package ${pname}\";\n\n          # Only install the binary for this specific package\n          # This avoids the output containing binaries from other crates\n        });\n\n        # Build all workspace members (useful for CI)\n        workspace-all = craneLib.buildPackage (commonArgs // {\n          inherit cargoArtifacts;\n          pname = \"my-workspace\";\n        });\n\n        # Individual binaries\n        server = buildWorkspaceBin \"server\";\n        cli = buildWorkspaceBin \"cli\";\n      in\n      {\n        checks = {\n          inherit workspace-all;\n\n          clippy = craneLib.cargoClippy (commonArgs // {\n            inherit cargoArtifacts;\n            cargoClippyExtraArgs = \"--workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\";\n          });\n\n          fmt = craneLib.cargoFmt { inherit src; };\n\n          tests = craneLib.cargoNextest (commonArgs // {\n            inherit cargoArtifacts;\n          });\n        };\n\n        packages = {\n          inherit server cli;\n          default = workspace-all;\n        };\n\n        apps = {\n          server = flake-utils.lib.mkApp { drv = server; };\n          cli = flake-utils.lib.mkApp { drv = cli; };\n          default = flake-utils.lib.mkApp { drv = server; };\n        };\n\n        devShells.default = craneLib.devShell {\n          checks = self.checks.${system};\n          packages = with pkgs; [\n            cargo-watch\n            cargo-edit\n          ];\n        };\n      }\n    );\n}\n```\n\n**Key workspace patterns:**\n\n- `buildDepsOnly` compiles all workspace dependencies once. The `cargoArtifacts` are then shared across all individual binary builds.\n- `cargoExtraArgs = \"--package ${pname}\"` is the correct way to target a specific workspace member (equivalent to `cargo build -p server`). You can also use `\"--bin binary-name\"` if a single crate produces multiple binaries.\n- `workspace-all` builds everything and is useful as a CI check.\n- Each binary gets its own `packages` entry so you can do `nix build .#server` or `nix build .#cli`.\n\n---\n\n### Example C: Without flake-utils (DIY forAllSystems)\n\nFor those who prefer no helper dependency:\n\n```nix\n{\n  description = \"Rust project without flake-utils\";\n\n  inputs = {\n    nixpkgs.url = \"github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable\";\n    crane.url = \"github:ipetkov/crane\";\n    rust-overlay = {\n      url = \"github:oxalica/rust-overlay\";\n      inputs.nixpkgs.follows = \"nixpkgs\";\n    };\n  };\n\n  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, crane, rust-overlay, ... }:\n    let\n      supportedSystems = [ \"x86_64-linux\" \"aarch64-linux\" \"x86_64-darwin\" \"aarch64-darwin\" ];\n      forAllSystems = f: nixpkgs.lib.genAttrs supportedSystems (system: f {\n        inherit system;\n        pkgs = import nixpkgs {\n          inherit system;\n          overlays = [ rust-overlay.overlays.default ];\n        };\n      });\n    in\n    {\n      packages = forAllSystems ({ pkgs, system }: let\n        rustToolchain = pkgs.rust-bin.stable.latest.default.override {\n          extensions = [ \"rust-src\" \"rust-analyzer\" \"clippy\" \"rustfmt\" ];\n        };\n        craneLib = (crane.mkLib pkgs).overrideToolchain rustToolchain;\n        src = craneLib.cleanCargoSource ./.;\n        commonArgs = { inherit src; strictDeps = true; };\n        cargoArtifacts = craneLib.buildDepsOnly commonArgs;\n      in {\n        default = craneLib.buildPackage (commonArgs // {\n          inherit cargoArtifacts;\n        });\n      });\n\n      devShells = forAllSystems ({ pkgs, system }: let\n        rustToolchain = pkgs.rust-bin.stable.latest.default.override {\n          extensions = [ \"rust-src\" \"rust-analyzer\" \"clippy\" \"rustfmt\" ];\n        };\n      in {\n        default = pkgs.mkShell {\n          packages = [ rustToolchain ] ++ (with pkgs; [\n            cargo-watch\n            cargo-edit\n          ]);\n        };\n      });\n    };\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Example D: Using a rust-toolchain.toml file\n\nIf your project has a `rust-toolchain.toml` (common in teams), you can read it directly. This works with **fenix** (which has first-class support for this) or with rust-overlay:\n\n**rust-toolchain.toml:**\n```toml\n[toolchain]\nchannel = \"1.83.0\"\ncomponents = [\"rust-src\", \"rust-analyzer\", \"clippy\", \"rustfmt\"]\ntargets = [\"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu\"]\n```\n\n**With rust-overlay:**\n```nix\nrustToolchain = pkgs.rust-bin.fromRustupToolchainFile ./rust-toolchain.toml;\ncraneLib = (crane.mkLib pkgs).overrideToolchain rustToolchain;\n```\n\n**With fenix:**\n```nix\nrustToolchain = fenix.packages.${system}.fromToolchainFile {\n  file = ./rust-toolchain.toml;\n  sha256 = \"sha256-AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA=\";\n};\ncraneLib = (crane.mkLib pkgs).overrideToolchain rustToolchain;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary of Recommendations\n\n| Decision | Recommendation | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Build system** | Crane | Incremental dep caching via `buildDepsOnly`, active maintenance, built-in clippy/fmt/test checks |\n| **Toolchain overlay** | oxalica/rust-overlay | Better maintained (devenv switched to it), simpler API, no hash required for stable |\n| **When to use fenix** | Nightly toolchains, `fromToolchainFile` | Slightly better nightly support; if your project pins a `rust-toolchain.toml` with nightly |\n| **Multi-system helper** | DIY `forAllSystems` or `flake-utils` | DIY eliminates a dependency; `flake-utils` is fine for smaller projects; `flake-parts` for large modular configs |\n| **Workspace binaries** | `cargoExtraArgs = \"--package name\"` | Share one `cargoArtifacts` across all workspace members; build each binary as a separate Nix package |\n| **Dev tooling** | Include in `extensions` on the toolchain | Put `rust-src`, `rust-analyzer`, `clippy`, `rustfmt` in the toolchain override so they are version-matched |\n| **CI checks** | Use `checks` flake output | `cargoClippy`, `cargoFmt`, `cargoNextest` as separate checks; `nix flake check` runs all |\n\n### Critical Details Often Missed\n\n1. **Always include `rust-src` in extensions.** Without it, rust-analyzer cannot navigate into standard library source code.\n\n2. **Use `strictDeps = true`** in all crane build calls. This prevents impure build inputs from leaking in.\n\n3. **Darwin build inputs.** If targeting macOS, you almost always need `darwin.apple_sdk.frameworks.Security` and `SystemConfiguration` for anything using TLS/networking.\n\n4. **`cleanCargoSource`** is important. It filters your source tree to only include `Cargo.toml`, `Cargo.lock`, and `*.rs` files, which dramatically improves Nix store caching (changing docs or CI configs will not trigger rebuilds).\n\n5. **`craneLib.devShell` vs `pkgs.mkShell`.** Crane's `devShell` automatically provides the toolchain and can inherit from `checks`. If you use `pkgs.mkShell`, you need to add the toolchain manually.</result>\n<usage>total_tokens: 16155\ntool_uses: 13\nduration_ms: 160725</usage>\n</task-notification>\nFull transcript available at: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-johnw-src-org2jsonl/tasks/a6f6d76.output",
